WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.450
Sixty-four people for the field trip.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.460
A minibus seats fourteen for three

3
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.420
hundred coins; a bus seats thirty

4
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.960
for five hundred and fifty.

5
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.880
The linear program says: hire

6
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.600
two point one three buses.

7
00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:16.870
Try telling that to the bus company.

8
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.790
Today: integer programs, and how branch

9
00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.190
and bound finds whole-number answers.

10
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.670
Drop the whole-number rule and you get

11
00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.790
the LP relaxation: one thousand one

12
00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.430
hundred and seventy-three coins.

13
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.060
No whole-number plan can be

14
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.060
cheaper, because the relaxation

15
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.740
allows more plans, so it’s a bound.

16
00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.790
But its answer can’t be used as it is.

17
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.770
Round down to two buses, and

18
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.130
four people stay behind.

19
00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.570
Round up to three buses, and it

20
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.330
costs sixteen hundred and fifty.

21
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.510
The best whole-number plan is neither: two

22
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.230
buses and a minibus, fourteen hundred.

23
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.060
Branch and bound searches cleverly.

24
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.270
Pick a variable with a fraction, like two

25
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.750
point one three buses, and split: at most

26
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.990
two buses, or at least three.

27
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.040
Each branch is a linear program again.

28
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.140
Bound: once you hold a whole-number plan,

29
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.140
any branch whose relaxation already costs

30
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.360
more is cut off unexplored.

31
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.600
The three-bus branch costs at least

32
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.380
sixteen fifty, more than the fourteen

33
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.140
hundred we have, so it’s pruned.

34
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.170
Solvers prove the best this way, often

35
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.870
without visiting most of the tree.

36
00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.960
While it runs, a solver reports

37
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.640
two numbers: the best plan so

38
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.600
far, and the best bound.

39
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.220
The gap between them says how

40
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.840
far from proven you are.

41
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.100
Here it starts at about sixteen percent,

42
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.180
and at zero, the plan is optimal.

43
00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.060
Quick check!

44
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.670
A cost-minimising model’s relaxation costs

45
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:06.990
one thousand, and the best whole-number

46
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.750
plan found so far costs twelve hundred.

47
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.650
What do you know about the true best?

48
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.890
It lies somewhere between the two.

49
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.840
In general, integer programming is

50
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.600
NP-hard: nobody knows a method that

51
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.380
is fast on every problem.

52
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.230
Yet solvers like SCIP, HiGHS, Gurobi and

53
00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.590
CP-SAT solve real problems with huge

54
00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.870
numbers of variables, thanks to good

55
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.430
bounds, cutting planes and heuristics.

56
00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.390
Yes-or-no variables model logic.

57
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.540
To charge a booking fee only if you

58
00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.260
hire any minibus, add a yes-or-no

59
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.500
variable, book, and the limit:

60
00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.400
minibuses at most ten times book.

61
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.460
If book is zero, no minibus;

62
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.460
if it’s one, up to ten.

63
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.100
In the app, open the truck load model.

64
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.050
Change the capacity, add a rule

65
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.830
of your own, and watch how the

66
00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.230
solver’s answer changes.

67
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.380
Your turn.

68
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.380
The minibus company adds a

69
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.180
three-hundred-coin booking fee.

70
00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.050
Are two buses and a minibus

71
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:18.810
still the cheapest?

72
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.600
No: with the fee it costs seventeen

73
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.120
hundred, so three buses at sixteen fifty

74
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:26.440
win.

75
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.880
Remember: the relaxation gives a

76
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.320
bound, and rounding can fail.

77
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.690
Branch and bound splits on

78
00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.090
fractions and prunes what can’t win.

79
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.140
And the gap tells you how

80
00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.400
close to proven you are.

81
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.140
Try it yourself: model the trip in

82
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.600
SCIP, with the booking fee.

83
00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.640
Then change the fee, and find the

84
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.360
fee at which the answer flips.
